 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.04 — The Gauss Map and the Second Fundamental Form\n\n3.03 measured everything an ant on the surface could measure. This notebook measures what the ant cannot see: how the surface **bends in the surrounding space**.\n\nThe instrument is the **Gauss map**\n\n$$n : S \\to S^2, \\qquad p \\mapsto \\text{the unit normal at } p,$$\n\nwhich records the direction the surface faces. A plane has a constant normal, so its Gauss map is a single point; a sphere of radius $R$ has $n(p) = \\pm p/R$, so its Gauss map covers all of $S^2$. **How fast $n$ varies is how much the surface curves.**\n\nDifferentiating gives, at each point, a linear map on the tangent plane. Since $|n| = 1$, differentiating $n\\cdot n = 1$ shows $dn_p$ takes values in $n^\\perp = T_pS$ — so it is a map\n\n$$S_p = -\\,dn_p : T_pS \\to T_pS,$$\n\nthe **shape operator** (or Weingarten map).\n\n**A warning about signs, since they cause endless confusion.** A surface has two unit normals, and choosing one is a choice of *orientation*. Flipping $n$ flips $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ and hence $S$, so:\n\n- the **principal curvatures $\\kappa_i$ and the mean curvature $H$ change sign** with the choice;\n- the **Gaussian curvature $K = \\det S$ does not**, because negating a $2\\times2$ matrix leaves its determinant alone.\n\nSo $K$ is the more robust invariant, and the familiar statement \"the unit sphere has $\\kappa_1 = \\kappa_2 = 1$\" implicitly uses the **inward** normal. The parametrisations below produce outward normals from $\\sigma_u \\times \\sigma_v$, so their $\\kappa_i$ come out negative — we verify the flip explicitly rather than quietly adjusting.\n\nTwo facts do the real work:\n\n**$S_p$ is self-adjoint** with respect to the first fundamental form: $\\langle S_pX, Y\\rangle = \\langle X, S_pY\\rangle$. So by the spectral theorem of 1.06 it is diagonalisable with real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigendirections — the **principal curvatures** and **principal directions**.\n\n**Its quadratic form is the second fundamental form:**\n\n$$\\mathrm{I\\!I} = \\begin{pmatrix} L & M \\\\ M & N\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad L = \\sigma_{uu}\\cdot n, \\quad M = \\sigma_{uv}\\cdot n, \\quad N = \\sigma_{vv}\\cdot n,$$\n\nand the shape operator is the ratio of the two forms, $S = \\mathrm{I}^{-1}\\mathrm{I\\!I}$. This is 1.06 again — a symmetric matrix whose eigenvalues are the geometry — but with *two* forms in play instead of one.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Normal curvature, and where $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ comes from\n\nTake a curve $\\gamma$ on the surface through $p$, parametrised by arc length. Its acceleration $\\gamma''$ splits into a part tangent to the surface and a part along the normal — the same tangential/normal split used for $\\gamma'' = v'T + v^2\\kappa N$ in 3.01, now with \"normal\" meaning normal *to the surface*. The normal component is\n\n$$\\kappa_n = \\gamma'' \\cdot n,$$\n\nthe **normal curvature**, and it measures how much the curve is forced to bend simply by being confined to the surface.\n\nDifferentiating $\\gamma' \\cdot n = 0$ along the curve gives $\\gamma''\\cdot n = -\\gamma'\\cdot n'$, so\n\n$$\\kappa_n = \\langle S_p\\gamma', \\gamma'\\rangle = \\mathrm{I\\!I}(\\gamma', \\gamma').$$\n\nTwo consequences follow at once, and the first is genuinely surprising:\n\n**Meusnier's theorem.** $\\kappa_n$ depends only on the *direction* $\\gamma'$, not on the curve. Every curve through $p$ in a given tangent direction has the same normal curvature — the surface dictates it.\n\n**Euler's formula.** Since $S_p$ is self-adjoint, write a unit tangent direction as $X = \\cos\\theta\\,e_1 + \\sin\\theta\\,e_2$ in the principal directions. Then\n\n$$\\kappa_n(\\theta) = \\kappa_1\\cos^2\\theta + \\kappa_2\\sin^2\\theta,$$\n\nwhich interpolates between $\\kappa_1$ and $\\kappa_2$. So **the principal curvatures are the maximum and minimum of the normal curvature over all directions**, attained in perpendicular directions.\n\n**Computing the shape operator.** In the basis $\\{\\sigma_u, \\sigma_v\\}$,\n\n$$S = \\mathrm{I}^{-1}\\,\\mathrm{I\\!I} = \\begin{pmatrix}E&F\\\\F&G\\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}L&M\\\\M&N\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nNote $S$ itself is generally *not* symmetric as a matrix — it is self-adjoint with respect to $\\mathrm{I}$, not with respect to the standard dot product on coordinates. Its eigenvalues are nonetheless real, and that is what matters. Both principal curvatures are visible in\n\n$$K = \\det S = \\frac{LN - M^2}{EG - F^2}, \\qquad H = \\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}S = \\frac{EN - 2FM + GL}{2(EG-F^2)},$$\n\nthe **Gaussian** and **mean** curvatures of 3.05.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s, var)  := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\n\n/* On 0 < u < π we have sin u > 0, so |sin u| = sin u.  Maxima will not\n   discharge this on its own, so rewrite it explicitly. */\nts(e) := ratsubst( sin(u), abs(sin(u)), trigsimp(e) )$\ntsm(m):= matrixmap(ts, m)$\n\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\n\nunitnormal(s,u_,v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  map(ts, n / sqrt(ts(n . n)) ))$\n\nSFF(s,u_,v_) := block([nn, suu, suv, svv],\n  nn  : unitnormal(s,u_,v_),\n  suu : pd(pd(s,u_),u_),  suv : pd(pd(s,u_),v_),  svv : pd(pd(s,v_),v_),\n  matrix([ts(suu . nn), ts(suv . nn)], [ts(suv . nn), ts(svv . nn)]) )$\n\nshape(s,u_,v_)  := tsm( invert(FFF(s,u_,v_)) . SFF(s,u_,v_) )$\nprinc(s,u_,v_)  := map(ts, eigenvalues(shape(s,u_,v_))[1] )$\nKgauss(s,u_,v_) := ts( determinant(SFF(s,u_,v_)) / determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_)) )$\nHmean(s,u_,v_)  := ts( mat_trace(shape(s,u_,v_))/2 )$\n\nassume(R > 0, a > 0, b > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\nplane    : [u, v, 0]$\ncylinder : [R*cos(u), R*sin(u), v]$\nsphere   : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\n\nfor su in [[\"plane   \", plane], [\"cylinder\", cylinder], [\"sphere  \", sphere]] do\n  block([],\n    print(su[1], \":\"),\n    print(\"     n  = \", unitnormal(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"     I  = \", FFF(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"     II = \", SFF(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"     S  = \", shape(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"     principal curvatures \", princ(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"     K = \", Kgauss(su[2],u,v), \"     H = \", Hmean(su[2],u,v)),\n    print(\"\") )$\n\nprint(\"The normal from σ_u × σ_v happens to point INWARD for these\")$\nprint(\"parametrisations, which is why κ₁, κ₂ and H come out negative.\")$\nprint(\"Flipping n negates II, hence S, hence H — but NOT K = det S,\")$\nprint(\"since a 2×2 determinant is unchanged by negating the matrix.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "plane    :\n     n  =  [0,0,1]\n     I  =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n     II =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n     S  =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n     principal curvatures  [0]\n     K =  0      H =  0\ncylinder :\n     n  =  [cos(u),sin(u),0]\n     I  =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,1])\n     II =  matrix([-R,0],[0,0])\n     S  =  matrix([-(1/R),0],[0,0])\n     principal curvatures  [-(1/R),0]\n     K =  0      H =  -(1/(2*R))\nsphere   :\n     n  =  [sin(u)*cos(v),sin(u)*sin(v),cos(u)]\n     I  =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\n     II =  matrix([-R,0],[0,-(R*sin(u)^2)])\n     S  =  matrix([-(1/R),0],[0,-(1/R)])\n     principal curvatures  [-(1/R)]\n     K =  1/R^2      H =  -(1/R)\nThe normal from σ_u × σ_v happens to point INWARD for these\nparametrisations, which is why κ₁, κ₂ and H come out negative.\nFlipping n negates II, hence S, hence H — but NOT K = det S,\nsince a 2×2 determinant is unchanged by negating the matrix."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Confirm the orientation claim: is σ_u × σ_v outward or inward? */\nn_sph : unitnormal(sphere, u, v)$\npos   : sphere/R$                    /* the outward radial direction */\nprint(\"sphere: unit normal from σ_u × σ_v = \", n_sph)$\nprint(\"        outward radial direction   = \", map(ts, pos))$\nprint(\"        difference = \", map(ts, n_sph - pos), \"  ⟹ OUTWARD\")$\n\nn_cyl : unitnormal(cylinder, u, v)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"cylinder: normal = \", n_cyl, \"  — also points radially outward\")$\n\n/* Flip the orientation and watch what changes */\nsphere_flip : [R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*cos(u)]$  /* swaps u,v roles */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"reversing orientation (by swapping the parameter order):\")$\nprint(\"   n      = \", unitnormal(sphere_flip, u, v))$\nprint(\"   S      = \", shape(sphere_flip, u, v))$\nprint(\"   κ      = \", princ(sphere_flip, u, v), \"   — now POSITIVE\")$\nprint(\"   H      = \", Hmean(sphere_flip, u, v), \"   — sign flipped\")$\nprint(\"   K      = \", Kgauss(sphere_flip, u, v), \"   — UNCHANGED ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"K = det S is the orientation-independent invariant; H is not.\")$\nprint(\"This is why Gaussian curvature, not mean curvature, is the one that\")$\nprint(\"turns out to be intrinsic (3.06) and appears in Gauss–Bonnet (3.08).\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "sphere: unit normal from σ_u × σ_v =  [sin(u)*cos(v),sin(u)*sin(v),cos(u)]\n        outward radial direction   =  [sin(u)*cos(v),sin(u)*sin(v),cos(u)]\n        difference =  [0,0,0]   ⟹ OUTWARD\ncylinder: normal =  [cos(u),sin(u),0]   — also points radially outward\nreversing orientation (by swapping the parameter order):\n   n      =  [-(sin(u)*sin(v)),-(sin(u)*cos(v)),-cos(u)]\n   S      =  matrix([1/R,0],[0,1/R])\n   κ      =  [1/R]    — now POSITIVE\n   H      =  1/R    — sign flipped\n   K      =  1/R^2    — UNCHANGED ✓\nK = det S is the orientation-independent invariant; H is not.\nThis is why Gaussian curvature, not mean curvature, is the one that\nturns out to be intrinsic (3.06) and appears in Gauss–Bonnet (3.08)."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Normal curvature in a given tangent direction, and Euler's formula --- */\nnormal_curv(s, u_, v_, w) := block([I1, I2],\n  I1 : FFF(s,u_,v_),  I2 : SFF(s,u_,v_),\n  ts( (I2[1][1]*w[1]^2 + 2*I2[1][2]*w[1]*w[2] + I2[2][2]*w[2]^2)\n      /(I1[1][1]*w[1]^2 + 2*I1[1][2]*w[1]*w[2] + I1[2][2]*w[2]^2) ) )$\n\n/* The saddle z = u² − v², at the origin */\nsaddle : [u, v, u^2 - v^2]$\nprint(\"saddle z = u² − v² at the origin:\")$\nS0 : subst([u=0, v=0], shape(saddle,u,v))$\nprint(\"   I  = \", subst([u=0,v=0], FFF(saddle,u,v)))$\nprint(\"   II = \", subst([u=0,v=0], SFF(saddle,u,v)))$\nprint(\"   S  = \", S0)$\nprint(\"   principal curvatures = \", eigenvalues(S0)[1])$\nprint(\"   K = \", subst([u=0,v=0], Kgauss(saddle,u,v)),\n      \"     H = \", subst([u=0,v=0], Hmean(saddle,u,v)))$\nprint(\"   K < 0: the surface curves opposite ways in the two directions\")$\n\n/* Euler's formula: sweep the direction and compare against κ₁cos²θ + κ₂sin²θ */\nk1 : 2$  k2 : -2$      /* the principal curvatures just computed */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   θ        κ_n computed        κ₁cos²θ + κ₂sin²θ\")$\nfor j : 0 thru 4 do block([th, w, kn, eu],\n  th : float(j*%pi/8),\n  w  : [cos(th), sin(th)],\n  kn : float( subst([u=0,v=0], normal_curv(saddle,u,v,w)) ),\n  eu : float( k1*cos(th)^2 + k2*sin(th)^2 ),\n  print(\"  \", th, \"     \", kn, \"      \", eu) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Euler's formula holds; κ_n runs between κ₂ = −2 and κ₁ = 2,\")$\nprint(\"hitting zero at θ = π/4 — the asymptotic directions.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "saddle z = u² − v² at the origin:\n   I  =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n   II =  matrix([2,0],[0,-2])\n   S  =  matrix([2,0],[0,-2])\n   principal curvatures =  [-2,2]\n   K =  -4      H =  0\n   K < 0: the surface curves opposite ways in the two directions\n   θ        κ_n computed        κ₁cos²θ + κ₂sin²θ\n   0.0       2.0        2.0\n   0.39269908169872414       1.414213562373097        1.414213562373095\n   0.7853981633974483       4.440892098500626e-16        4.440892098500626e-16\n   1.1780972450961724       -1.4142135623730965        -1.414213562373095\n   1.5707963267948966       -2.0        -2.0\nEuler's formula holds; κ_n runs between κ₂ = −2 and κ₁ = 2,\nhitting zero at θ = π/4 — the asymptotic directions."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The torus: curvature of both signs on one surface --- */\n/* A genuine (non-self-intersecting) torus needs a > b, so a + b cos u > 0. */\nfacts2 : assume(a > b)$\ntorus : [(a + b*cos(u))*cos(v), (a + b*cos(u))*sin(v), b*sin(u)]$\nKt : Kgauss(torus, u, v)$\nprint(\"torus (a + b cos u), with a > b:\")$\nprint(\"   K = \", Kt, \"   — the sign of K is the sign of cos u\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"      u             position          cos u        K (a=3,b=1)\")$\nfor uu in [0, %pi/3, %pi/2, 2*%pi/3, %pi] do\n  print(\"   \", float(uu), \"   \",\n        if uu = 0 then \"outer equator\"\n        elseif uu = %pi then \"inner equator\"\n        elseif uu = %pi/2 then \"top circle   \"\n        else \"             \",\n        \"   \", float(cos(uu)), \"   \",\n        float( subst([a=3,b=1,u=uu], Kt) ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   outer half (cos u > 0): K > 0, dome-like\")$\nprint(\"   top and bottom circles (cos u = 0): K = 0\")$\nprint(\"   inner half (cos u < 0): K < 0, saddle-like\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"So a single smooth surface carries all three curvature signs —\")$\nprint(\"which is exactly what Stage 2's three homogeneous geometries could\")$\nprint(\"not do, and the reason curvature had to become a function.\")$\n\n/* Total curvature.  The integrand collapses completely: since a + b cos u > 0,\n   √((a+b cos u)²) = a + b cos u and everything cancels. */\ndA    : ts( sqrt(determinant(FFF(torus,u,v))) )$\nKdA   : ratsimp( Kt * dA )$\nKdA2  : ratsubst( a + b*cos(u), sqrt(b^2*cos(u)^2 + 2*a*b*cos(u) + a^2), KdA )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"area element  = \", dA)$\nprint(\"K · dA        = \", KdA)$\nprint(\"              = \", ratsimp(KdA2), \"   — it is just cos u du dv\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"∫₀^{2π}∫₀^{2π} cos u du dv = \",\n      integrate( integrate( cos(u), u, 0, 2*%pi ), v, 0, 2*%pi ))$\nprint(\"   — zero, and the torus has Euler characteristic χ = 0.\")$\nprint(\"   Gauss–Bonnet says ∫∫ K dA = 2πχ; this is a preview of 3.08.\")$\nprint(\"   Note the total is independent of a and b: it cannot be otherwise,\")$\nprint(\"   since it is fixed by the topology alone.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 7,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "torus (a + b cos u), with a > b:\n   K =  cos(u)/(b^2*cos(u)+a*b)    — the sign of K is the sign of cos u\n      u             position          cos u        K (a=3,b=1)\n    0.0     outer equator     1.0     0.25\n    1.0471975511965976                       0.5     0.14285714285714285\n    1.5707963267948966     top circle        0.0     0.0\n    2.0943951023931953                       -0.5     -0.2\n    3.141592653589793     inner equator     -1.0     -0.5\n   outer half (cos u > 0): K > 0, dome-like\n   top and bottom circles (cos u = 0): K = 0\n   inner half (cos u < 0): K < 0, saddle-like\nSo a single smooth surface carries all three curvature signs —\nwhich is exactly what Stage 2's three homogeneous geometries could\nnot do, and the reason curvature had to become a function.\narea element  =  b*sqrt(b^2*cos(u)^2+2*a*b*cos(u)+a^2)\nK · dA        =  (cos(u)*sqrt(b^2*cos(u)^2+2*a*b*cos(u)+a^2))/(b*cos(u)+a)\n              =  cos(u)    — it is just cos u du dv\n∫₀^{2π}∫₀^{2π} cos u du dv =  0\n   — zero, and the torus has Euler characteristic χ = 0.\n   Gauss–Bonnet says ∫∫ K dA = 2πχ; this is a preview of 3.08.\n   Note the total is independent of a and b: it cannot be otherwise,\n   since it is fixed by the topology alone."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Normal curvature as the tangent direction sweeps around, for three surfaces */\nsweep(s, u0, v0) := block([ths],\n  ths : makelist(float(2*%pi*k/240), k, 0, 240),\n  [discrete, ths,\n   map(lambda([th], float( subst([u=u0, v=v0],\n        normal_curv(s, u, v, [cos(th), sin(th)]) ))), ths)] )$\n\n/* saddle at the origin: κ = ±2, so κ_n swings through zero      */\n/* sphere R=1: umbilic, κ_n constant                              */\n/* torus at u=π/3 (a=3,b=1): both principal curvatures positive   */\nsph1  : subst(R=1, sphere)$\ntor31 : subst([a=3,b=1], torus)$\nprint(\"saddle at origin   : principal curvatures \",\n      float(eigenvalues(subst([u=0,v=0], shape(saddle,u,v)))[1]))$\nprint(\"unit sphere        : principal curvatures \",\n      float(subst(R=1, princ(sphere,u,v))), \"  (umbilic — every direction alike)\")$\nprint(\"torus at u = π/3   : principal curvatures \",\n      float(subst(u=%pi/3, princ(tor31,u,v))))$\n\nplot2d([ sweep(saddle, 0, 0),\n         sweep(sph1, %pi/3, 0),\n         sweep(tor31, %pi/3, 0),\n         [discrete, [0.0, 6.3], [0.0, 0.0]] ],\n       [x, 0, 6.3], [y, -2.5, 2.5],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,2],[lines,2],[lines,1]],\n       [legend, \"saddle (K < 0)\", \"sphere (umbilic)\", \"torus at u = π/3\", \"zero\"],\n       [xlabel, \"direction angle θ\"], [ylabel, \"normal curvature κ_n\"],\n       [title, \"Euler: κ_n = κ₁cos²θ + κ₂sin²θ, swinging between the two extremes\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 8,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "saddle at origin   : principal curvatures  [-2.0,2.0]\nunit sphere        : principal curvatures\n  [-1.0]   (umbilic — every direction alike)\ntorus at u = π/3   : principal curvatures  [0.14285714285714285,1.0]"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
