  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Surface | $\\kappa_1, \\kappa_2$ | $K$ | $H$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| plane | $0, 0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| cylinder (radius $R$) | $-1/R,\\; 0$ | $0$ | $-1/(2R)$ |\n| sphere (radius $R$, outward $n$) | $-1/R,\\; -1/R$ | $+1/R^2$ | $-1/R$ |\n| sphere (inward $n$) | $+1/R,\\; +1/R$ | $+1/R^2$ | $+1/R$ |\n| saddle $z = u^2-v^2$ at $0$ | $2, -2$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n| torus, outer half | both same sign | $> 0$ | — |\n| torus, inner half | opposite signs | $< 0$ | — |\n\nVerified: $S = \\mathrm{I}^{-1}\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ computed for each; Euler's formula $\\kappa_n = \\kappa_1\\cos^2\\theta + \\kappa_2\\sin^2\\theta$ matched a direct computation of normal curvature at five angles on the saddle, passing through zero at $\\theta = \\pi/4$; reversing orientation flipped $\\kappa_i$ and $H$ while leaving $K$ untouched; and the torus's $K\\,dA$ collapsed exactly to $\\cos u \\, du\\,dv$, integrating to **zero** over the whole surface.\n\n**The cylinder is the notebook in miniature.** It has $\\kappa_1 = -1/R \\ne 0$, so it genuinely bends in space — yet $K = \\kappa_1\\kappa_2 = 0$, the same as a plane. And 3.03 showed it is intrinsically flat. The *product* of the principal curvatures is blind to the bending that a single one detects. That is the first hint of the Theorema Egregium.\n\n**The torus is the other lesson.** One smooth surface carries $K > 0$, $K = 0$ and $K < 0$ on different parts of itself. No homogeneous model can do that, which is precisely why Stage 2's framework had to be replaced by a pointwise theory.\n\n## What is set up for the next notebooks\n\n- **3.05** interprets $K$ and $H$ separately: $K$ classifies points as elliptic, hyperbolic or parabolic; $H = 0$ characterises minimal surfaces.\n- **3.06** proves the *Theorema Egregium*: although $K$ was defined here from $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$, which needs the ambient space, it is in fact computable from $\\mathrm{I}$ alone. The cylinder's $K = 0$ then stops being a coincidence and becomes a necessity, given that it is intrinsically a plane.\n- **3.08** integrates $K$ over a closed surface and finds $2\\pi\\chi$ — the total curvature of the torus computed above being the case $\\chi = 0$.\n\nNote the pattern in that list: $K$ was introduced extrinsically, will be proved intrinsic, and will finally be shown to be topological. Each step makes it less dependent on the embedding, and the last removes the geometry entirely.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §3-2 for the Gauss map and the second fundamental form; §3-3 for Euler's formula and the classification of points.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapters 7–8, with careful attention to the orientation conventions that trip people up here.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 8 treats the second fundamental form of a submanifold in the general setting, of which this is the case of a surface in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 5 for the shape operator and its eigenvalues.\n\nMeusnier's theorem dates from 1776, Euler's formula for normal curvature from 1760 — both predating Gauss's *Disquisitiones circa superficies curvas* (1827), which unified them.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}